  {
    "pkattachmentId": 71901,
    "bookid": 65,
    "chapterId": 101,
    "fileId": 35775,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Handling and processing of enucleated eyes with retinoblastoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<strong>Upper left</strong>&nbsp;After adequate fixation the eye is sectioned to obtain a central pupil&ndash;optic nerve section that contains the intact optic nerve (upper centre). The caps (calottes) (upper right and left) resulting from the sectioning are further serially sectioned anteriorly to posteriorly (lower left) to obtain segments of the calottes (lower right), maximizing the surface of choroid to be studied. <strong>Lower left</strong>&nbsp;The entire eye is then submitted in three&nbsp;cassettes for histological sectioning. The resulting slides show the segments of the calottes on the cut edge to evaluate the choroid and sclera and the central pupil&ndash;optic nerve to evaluate the optic nerve in relation to the intraocular tumour. <strong>Upper right</strong>&nbsp;Histopathological examination of several sections and levels of the central pupil&ndash;optic nerve segment is recommended to evaluate possible invasion and if present the depth of invasion into the optic nerve. Sections may be cut serially and stained every five&nbsp;levels keeping the unstained slides (bottom) for possible staining with immunohistochemistry or H&amp;E if necessary. <strong>Lower right</strong>&nbsp;Sectioning should continue until the complete centre of the optic nerve is seen on the slides as represented in this microphotograph of the optic nerve. Notice the central vessels in the optic nerve and in the retina/tumour, and the presence of the lamina cribrosa (LC). The tumour is barely touching the inner layers of the lamina cribrosa in this example.",
    "source": "Patricia Chevez-Barrios",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/65/101/35775.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/65/101/thumb_35775.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 71902,
    "bookid": 65,
    "chapterId": 101,
    "fileId": 35777,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Vitreous retinoblastoma tumour seeding",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<strong>Left</strong>&nbsp;The background shows dust-type vitreous seeding, where there is a combination of necrotic cells, macrophages, and scattered single tumour cells. The nests of floating tumour cells are sphere type&nbsp;1, which are those that have solid centres and clinically appear as translucent spheres. Notice the small budding tumour cells that detach from the main sphere and create new tumour seeds. <strong>Middle</strong>&nbsp;Sphere type&nbsp;2 tumour seeding shows a central area of necrosis and clinically these appear as spheres with a white or yellow centre. <strong>Right</strong>&nbsp;A close-up shows an area of cloud-type seeding in which most of the cloud corresponds to large areas of tumour necrosis (lower left), rimmed by viable spheres. In this example the cloud is behind and adjacent to the lens and the ciliary body (upper right). Notice the PAS-positive basement membranes of these structures.",
    "source": "Patricia Chevez-Barrios",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/65/101/35777.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/65/101/thumb_35777.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 71903,
    "bookid": 65,
    "chapterId": 101,
    "fileId": 35779,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Cytology of retinoblastoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<strong>Left</strong>&nbsp;FNA of tumour in the anterior chamber shows groups and rosettes of atypical cells with a high N:C&nbsp;ratio associated with apoptotic cells as seen in this Pap-stained liquid-based cytology. <strong>Middle</strong>&nbsp;An H&amp;E-stained touch imprint of a newly sectioned optic nerve near the true margin shows groups of meningothelial cells (top right), blood, and two atypical retinoblastoma cells with a high N:C&nbsp;ratio (lower left). <strong>Right</strong>&nbsp;Nearby the area of meningothelial cells there is a large group of retinoblastoma cells that have a rosette configuration.",
    "source": "Patricia Chevez-Barrios",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/65/101/35779.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/65/101/thumb_35779.jpg"
  },
